{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01Pa6bkHrR6JzzRtGaKTcQHQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So the testutils framework has a hardcoded 10-second timeout. This means I can't test `sleep infinity` directly because both binaries would block and the test would fail after 10 seconds with `t.Fatalf`.\n\nThe approach for testing R2.4 (infinity/inf) needs to be different. Looking at R4.4 which says tests must use \"short durations (0 or 0.01) to avoid slow test execution\", and R4.3 says \"Differential tests must compare exit codes between the Go binary and the reference binary.\"\n \nSince the testutils framework will kill both binaries after 10 seconds anyway, they'd both get terminated the same way—but the test itself would fail with a timeout error. So I can't use the standard differential testing approach for infinity. I'm weighing whether to skip the infinity test entirely (though R2.4 requires it), write a separate non-differential test, or find another solution.\n\nActually, since the test file is in the same package, I can access the unexported `parseDuration` function directly and write a unit test that verifies it accepts \"inf\" and \"infinity\" as valid inputs, which would satisfy the requirement without needing the differential framework. I'm realizing the blocking nature of `sleep inf` makes differential testing impossible with the framework's timeout behavior, so I'll add a unit test for `parseDuration` covering the \"inf\" and \"infinity\" cases, plus a simple subprocess test that starts the process and verifies it doesn't immediately error before killing it. I'm writing a test case for the parseDuration function to verify it correctly handles the \"inf\" and \"infinity\" string inputs.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1337,"cache_read_input_tokens":25241,"cache_creation":{"epheme